    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d24">

                <pb facs="0033" n="23" sameAs="frus:frus1949v09/pg23"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.01/5–1549: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-05-15T08:00:00+08:00">May 15, 1949—8 a.
                            m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 11:18 p. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1025. With reference paragraph 5, Embtel 1018 [<hi rendition="#italic">1019</hi>]
                    of May 13,<anchor n="48" xml:id="d24-f1"/> repeated Shanghai 568, Canton 411, we
                    suggest that when time finally does come to accord <hi rendition="#italic">de
                        jure</hi> recognition to Chinese Communists, Department may wish try make
                    such recognition contingent upon Communists <pb facs="0034" n="24" xml:id="pg24"
                    /> signing detailed agreement along lines 1933 Litvinov Agreement with
                        USSR.<anchor n="49" xml:id="d24-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>Some advantages which would accrue from this are: By pinning Communists down to
                    certain specific minimal obligations, it would reduce their scope to
                    misinterpret and quibble about generalities in manner characteristic of both
                    Communists and Chinese. It would impress them that we are not intimidated by
                    their truculence and that they and not we have most to gain by establishment of
                    diplomatic relations. It would dovetail with Embassy recommendation (Embtel 921,
                    May 3) that we do not take first step toward recognition. If agreement is
                    sufficiently detailed, it would give US convenient and specific references on
                    which to hang many protests against violations American rights which we must
                    expect in Communist China.</p>
                <p>Sent Department 1025, repeated Shanghai 574, OffEmb Canton 416.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
